     },
     "name": "VP Software Development and Analytics 1",
     "signPrefixType": "EMPTY",
     "type": "TITLE"
    },
    "commentType": "POST_COMMENT",
    "date": "2026-09-13T03:13:21.472Z",
    "id": "6aa614d18e8ca166d54b6ce5",
    "isAuthor": false,
    "messageData": {
     "imageUrls": null,
     "linkMetadata": null,
     "text": "As a VP of a fully remote team - I can see this being valuable. While I do not “play games” we do meet three times a week for 1 hour.  \n\nThe main problem with remote work and remote employees, they tend to work and think in silos - only work on what is assigned no more and no less.  What does the sprint board say. That is fine when you evaluate and value productivity by piece meal or story points.  I value productivity as a positive influence towards a common goal.  This common goal is set by leadership, management and team together.  This is missing in today’s environment. The water cooler talk, break room conversations, passing in the hall all had productive communications that do not happen any more. Teams seem to have just a vague understanding of their teammate - with my not caring anything about their teammates - they are removed, so not important. \n\nI cannot speak for your leadership but I am expecting that they are trying to get the team to see and understand they are part of a bigger picture and need to learn to communicate and trust in a non formal fashion - this is needed for true productivity.  \n\nIf you have good leadership, ask them why.  I love it when my team asks me why - You might not see why and they do.  Management does not always expose things well or sometimes intentional.  If you still think it is not needed, let them know and discuss.  Be nice and respectful with an open mind.  Be expected to finally hear because I want you too - after a couple of pushbacks. Then you can decide your voluntary future with the company."
    },
    "messageType": "TEXT",
    "myReaction": null,
